.SH "NAME"
yabai \- window manager
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
\fByabai\fP [\fB\-\-load\-sa\fP|\fB\-\-uninstall\-sa\fP|\fB\-\-install\-service\fP|\fB\-\-uninstall\-service\fP|\fB\-\-start\-service\fP|\fB\-\-restart\-service\fP|\fB\-\-stop\-service\fP|\fB\-\-doctor\fP|\fB\-\-message\fP,\fB\-m\fP \fImsg\fP|\fB\-\-config\fP,\fB\-c\fP \fIconfig_file\fP|\fB\-\-config\-jsonc\fP \fIconfig_file\fP|\fB\-\-check\-config\fP \fIconfig_file\fP|\fB\-\-verbose\fP,\fB\-V\fP|\fB\-\-version\fP,\fB\-v\fP|\fB\-\-help\fP,\fB\-h\fP]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
\fByabai\fP is a tiling window manager for macOS based on binary space partitioning.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-\-load\-sa\fP
.RS 4
Load the scripting\-addition into Dock.app.
.br
Installs and updates the scripting\-addition when necessary.
.br
Path is /Library/ScriptingAdditions/yabai.osax.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-uninstall\-sa\fP
.RS 4
Uninstall the scripting\-addition. Must be run as root.
.br
Path is /Library/ScriptingAdditions/yabai.osax.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-install\-service\fP
.RS 4
Writes a launchd service file to disk.
.br
Path is ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.asmvik.yabai.plist.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-uninstall\-service\fP
.RS 4
Removes a launchd service file from disk.
.br
Path is ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.asmvik.yabai.plist.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-start\-service\fP
.RS 4
Enables, loads, and starts the launchd service.
.br
Will install service file if it does not exist.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-restart\-service\fP
.RS 4
Attempts to restart the service instance.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-stop\-service\fP
.RS 4
Stops a running instance of the service and unloads it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-message\fP, \fB\-m\fP \fI<msg>\fP
.RS 4
Send message to a running instance of yabai.
.RE
.sp
Yabai stores its daemon socket, scripting\-addition socket, and their lifecycle
locks in \f(CR~/Library/Caches/yabai/runtime\fP. The directory is private mode 0700 and
socket and lock entries are mode 0600. Startup and client operations fail closed
if the home/cache path has unsafe ownership, permissions, ACLs, symlinks, or
conflicting entries; legacy shared \f(CR/tmp\fP entries are not used. These checks and
locks protect against other UIDs and coordinate cooperating instances of the
same UID. A malicious process running as the same UID can still mutate pathname
entries between non\-atomic Unix\-domain socket unlink and bind operations and is
outside this isolation boundary.
.sp
\fB\-\-config\fP, \fB\-c\fP \fI<config_file>\fP
.RS 4
Use the specified legacy \fByabairc\fP file.
.br
Executes the file directly if it is a regular file executable by the current user.
.br
If direct execution rejects the file format, or the file is not executable, interprets it using \f(CR/usr/bin/env sh <config_file>\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-config\-jsonc\fP \fI<config_file>\fP
.RS 4
Use the specified native JSONC configuration file. A relative path is captured as an absolute lexical path before startup.
.br
This option does not disable \fByabairc\fP discovery or an explicit \fB\-\-config\fP path.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-check\-config\fP \fI<config_file>\fP
.RS 4
Validate one native JSONC file and exit. This exact three\-argument invocation cannot be combined with any other option or trailing argument.
.br
Success prints \f(CRconfiguration is valid; runtime preparation was not checked\fP. It checks file input, JSONC syntax, schema, limits and regular expressions, but not display or Space targets, permissions, event resources, runtime labels or other runtime preparation.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-doctor\fP
.RS 4
Run bounded, read\-only installation diagnostics and exit. Each diagnostic is classified as \f(CRPASS\fP, \f(CRWARN\fP, \f(CRFAIL\fP, or \f(CRUNKNOWN\fP, names the inspection mechanism, and gives a next action. The checks cover the running binary, version, native slices and translation; Accessibility trust; core and optional scripting\-addition SIP requirements; the Apple Silicon arm64e boot argument, including when the x86_64 slice runs under Rosetta (omitted only on native Intel); canonical launch agent plist fields read from one no\-follow descriptor snapshot, CFBoolean types, safe type, ownership and mode, and a program that resolves to the running yabai executable (a matching path\-component symlink warns rather than passes, after both lexical and canonical parent chains are checked and the original path is re\-resolved with final identity, owner, group, mode and execute permission revalidated); the known \f(CRcom.koekeishiya.yabai.plist\fP and \f(CRhomebrew.mxcl.yabai.plist\fP collisions; selected native and legacy configuration readability; and descriptor\-snapshot running\-binary and scripting\-addition bundle/socket metadata. After socket ownership, mode, ACL, and type validation succeeds, doctor performs one bounded read\-only payload handshake to classify the live scripting\-addition version and required capabilities. Scripting\-addition absence is determined from the top\-level \f(CRyabai.osax\fP bundle; once that bundle is observed, missing or raced nested metadata is \f(CRUNKNOWN\fP, not absent. User paths and the socket name are derived from the real uid, or from a valid nonroot \f(CRSUDO_UID\fP when invoked as root, using the passwd database rather than \f(CRHOME\fP or \f(CRUSER\fP. During an elevated inspection, permissions are evaluated by forking a bounded probe, initializing the target user\(cqs supplementary groups, dropping to the target uid/gid, and calling \f(CRaccess(2)\fP, so effective ACL and mode\-bit policy is honored. If that target\-identity probe is unavailable, a conservative owner/group/other mode\-bit fallback uses the target uid plus primary and resolved supplementary groups; unavailable or unrepresentable group membership remains \f(CRUNKNOWN\fP rather than using root\(cqs own access result. Legacy configuration directories, including symlinks to directories, are skipped according to runtime precedence, while a selected non\-regular file fails. launchd loaded/running state and scripting\-addition responsiveness remain \f(CRUNKNOWN\fP because doctor does not contact either service. It does not prompt for permissions, contact or load the scripting addition, invoke \f(CRsudo\fP, change launchd services, or inspect skhd. Unavailable privileged information and transient inspection errors are \f(CRUNKNOWN\fP; only permission denial or a contractually absent/invalid state is definitive. Optional prerequisites are warnings. The exit status is zero when there are no \f(CRFAIL\fP results and nonzero otherwise. Output is deliberately limited and does not dump paths, the environment, plist contents, or configuration contents.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-verbose\fP, \fB\-V\fP
.RS 4
Output debug information to stdout.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-version\fP, \fB\-v\fP
.RS 4
Print version to stdout and exit.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-help\fP, \fB\-h\fP
.RS 4
Print options to stdout and exit.
.RE
.SH "NATIVE JSONC CONFIGURATION"
.SS "Startup and validation"
.sp
The native configuration file is named \f(CRyabai.jsonc\fP. Without \fB\-\-config\-jsonc\fP, yabai checks an absolute \f(CR$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yabai/yabai.jsonc\fP first, then \f(CR$HOME/.config/yabai/yabai.jsonc\fP. A relative \f(CRXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP is ignored. A missing discovered file is allowed; a missing explicit file is an error. Ordinary symlinks are followed. An explicit relative path is captured as an absolute lexical path without resolving its final symlink, and reload reopens that same path. If no native file was installed, reload repeats default discovery.
.sp
Native JSONC and \fByabairc\fP are independent. At daemon startup yabai parses and prepares JSONC, installs native settings and rules, inventories existing windows, publishes the initial menu state, starts the message socket, and then executes \fByabairc\fP. Native rules therefore apply to the initial window inventory. Settings in \fByabairc\fP override native settings, and rules added there follow the native rule prefix and normally affect later windows only.
.sp
Use the exact offline check before starting the daemon:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
yabai \-\-check\-config ~/.config/yabai/yabai.jsonc
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
This command validates the authored file and schema only. It does not resolve display or Space indices, check current topology, check runtime label collisions, test SIP or Screen Recording requirements, or acquire daemon resources. Startup and reload can still reject a file that passes this check.
.SS "File and JSONC rules"
.sp
The file must be a regular UTF\-8 file no larger than 1 MiB. One UTF\-8 BOM is accepted only at byte zero. \f(CR//\fP line comments and \f(CR/* ... */\fP block comments are accepted outside strings. Block comments do not nest. Trailing commas, single\-quoted strings, unquoted keys, JSON5 numbers and other JSON5 syntax are rejected.
.sp
The reader opens the selected path once with \f(CRO_RDONLY | O_CLOEXEC\fP, follows ordinary symlinks, and compares descriptor metadata before and after reading. A changed device, inode, file type, size, high\-resolution modification time or change time rejects the candidate. Premature EOF and detected growth also reject it. Unlocked POSIX reads cannot detect a same\-metadata overwrite, so this check promises detection of observable metadata changes rather than every concurrent write.
.sp
Raw NUL bytes, malformed UTF\-8, a BOM away from byte zero, UTF\-16 or UTF\-32 input, unpaired Unicode surrogates, and decoded U+0000 in any key or string are rejected. Decoded object keys must be unique within their object. Unknown keys and \f(CRnull\fP are rejected. Diagnostic JSON paths use known schema names, array indices and fixed placeholders; unknown or duplicate key text is not repeated.
.sp
Version 1 remains compatible and accepts exactly \f(CRversion\fP, \f(CRconfig\fP, \f(CRspaces\fP and \f(CRrules\fP. Version 2 accepts those fields plus \f(CRmodes\fP and \f(CRactive_mode\fP. \f(CRversion\fP is required and must have the mathematical integer value 1 or 2; \f(CRmodes\fP and \f(CRactive_mode\fP are rejected in version 1. All other fields shown here are optional:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
{
  "version": 2,
  "config": {},
  "spaces": [],
  "modes": [
    { "name": "work", "spaces": [] }
  ],
  "active_mode": "work",
  "rules": []
}
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "Numbers and limits"
.sp
Integer fields use mathematical value semantics: \f(CR1\fP, \f(CR1.0\fP and \f(CR1e0\fP are equivalent when the value is integral and in range. JSON booleans are not numbers. Signed setting integers use \f(CRINT_MIN...INT_MAX\fP; unsigned fields use the ranges stated below.
.sp
A float starts as a finite \f(CRdouble\fP. Except for an allowed zero, its absolute value must be between \f(CRFLT_MIN\fP and \f(CRFLT_MAX\fP. Yabai casts it once to \f(CRfloat\fP, requires the result to remain finite and nonzero when the input was nonzero, then applies the field range. Rounding inside that destination range is accepted.
.sp
