.sp
\f(CRexternal_bar\fP is an exact object. All three fields are required: \f(CRmode\fP is \f(CRoff\fP, \f(CRmain\fP or \f(CRall\fP, while \f(CRtop_padding\fP and \f(CRbottom_padding\fP are mathematical integers in \f(CRINT_MIN...INT_MAX\fP.
.sp
The native \f(CRwindow_shadow\fP, \f(CRwindow_opacity\fP, \f(CRwindow_opacity_duration\fP, \f(CRactive_window_opacity\fP and \f(CRnormal_window_opacity\fP settings use the scripting addition when yabai applies them to windows. The scripting addition requires partial SIP disablement. Other native settings do not gain that requirement merely by being stored in JSONC; \f(CRwindow_animation_duration\fP has the separate Screen Recording and SIP preflight described above.
.SS "Space policy"
.sp
Each \f(CRspaces\fP entry is an exact object with required \f(CRmatch\fP and at least one of \f(CRsettings\fP or \f(CRscaffold\fP. \f(CRmatch\fP contains exactly one selector: a positive global one\-based Mission Control \f(CRindex\fP, or a nonempty exact \f(CRuuid\fP. When present, \f(CRsettings\fP must contain at least one of \f(CRlayout\fP, \f(CRtop_padding\fP, \f(CRbottom_padding\fP, \f(CRleft_padding\fP, \f(CRright_padding\fP, \f(CRwindow_gap\fP, \f(CRaccordion_padding\fP, \f(CRsingle_window_width_ratio\fP, \f(CRsingle_window_min_width\fP, \f(CRsingle_window_max_width\fP, \f(CRsplit_ratio\fP, \f(CRsplit_type\fP and \f(CRauto_balance\fP, using the types and ranges above. A Space \f(CRsplit_ratio\fP uses the existing \f(CR0.1...0.9\fP range and controls the default ratio for new BSP splits on that Space.
.sp
Yabai evaluates Space entries in file order and resolves each setting separately. Later matching entries win within the native Space layer. A positive index that is not present in the current valid topology is accepted and its policy remains dormant. An index follows its Mission Control slot, so reconciliation automatically applies that policy if the slot later appears; a UUID follows the captured identity when macOS remaps its runtime Space ID. System and native\-fullscreen Spaces remain floating.
.sp
Effective precedence is:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
built\-in < native global < native Space < runtime global < runtime Space
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
A successful imperative \f(CRconfig\fP command owns the field at its runtime layer. Runtime ownership survives topology reconciliation, Space remapping, mode activation and mode deactivation, then clears only after a successful JSONC reload. When an installed policy has a scaffold, successful imperative global or scoped layout changes request coalesced reconciliation so a BSP scaffold is restored after a stack\-to\-BSP transition without changing inventory or runtime ownership. \f(CRspace \-\-layout\fP uses runtime Space ownership. For \f(CRwindow_focus_fallback\fP, this means \f(CRyabai \-m config window_focus_fallback recent\fP overrides the native value until a successful reload clears runtime ownership and reapplies the native candidate; omitting it from that candidate restores the built\-in \f(CRoff\fP value. Imperative \f(CRspace \-\-padding\fP, \f(CRspace \-\-gap\fP and their toggles do not create declarative ownership; a later native reconciliation may overwrite their numeric result.
.SS "Named modes"
.sp
Version 2 may declare up to 64 uniquely named modes in source order. A mode is an exact object containing \f(CRname\fP (a nonempty UTF\-8 string of at most 1,024 bytes) and \f(CRspaces\fP (an array using the version 2 Space\-policy form). Base \f(CRspaces\fP are evaluated first, then the selected mode\(cqs policies overlay them in declaration order. Thus later matching mode fields override native base fields while runtime global and runtime Space ownership still have higher precedence.
.sp
Every base or mode Space policy requires \f(CRmatch\fP and at least one of \f(CRsettings\fP or \f(CRscaffold\fP. For scaffold specifically, omission from a policy that has \f(CRsettings\fP means inherit the last matching base or earlier mode scaffold, while \f(CR"scaffold": []\fP explicitly clears it. A nonempty mode scaffold replaces the earlier scaffold. Missing applications simply leave their scaffold primary slots vacant; mode selection never launches applications.
.sp
\f(CRactive_mode\fP, when present, must exactly name a declared mode and selects it during startup and every successful reload. At runtime, \f(CRyabai \-m mode \-\-activate <name>\fP selects an overlay and \f(CR\-\-deactivate\fP selects base policies only; deactivation does not restore the file\(cqs \f(CRactive_mode\fP and does not clear imperative runtime overrides. Reload selects the newly authored \f(CRactive_mode\fP (or no mode when omitted) and, like every successful reload, clears runtime setting ownership under the normal reload contract.
.sp
Runtime selection is transactional and errors are typed through topology capture/snapshot and Space preparation/commit. An unknown explicit name, malformed internal state, or invariant/admission/commit failure is terminal; an unavailable error means no installed native mode runtime; allocation/resource exhaustion and a temporarily unavailable production Space inventory query may be retried later without reserving reconciliation; only topology settling, races, or stale identity failures reserve runtime\-owned reconciliation and are retried after it settles. A successful command reports selection publication, not completion of the follow\-up asynchronous reconciliation, and the client performs exactly one activation/deactivation operation with no retry loop. Failed selection produces no query/list JSON and preserves the installed selection, rules, runtime ownership and Space state.
.sp
\f(CR\-\-query\fP returns the active mode as one escaped JSON string, or JSON \f(CRnull\fP. \f(CR\-\-list\fP returns stable JSON with declared names in source order and \f(CRactive\fP set to that same string or \f(CRnull\fP. Snapshot creation is worker\-only and requires a fresh\-zeroed or previously destroyed output. It returns owned copies of the names array, each name and the optional active name; callers must destroy it. Queries therefore copy installed names before output so a later reload cannot invalidate borrowed model storage.
.sp
Modes do not export, import, save, capture or undo layouts; launch applications; mutate the native rule generation; or persist runtime selection back to the JSONC file. Their Phase 1 scope is declarative Space policy and scaffold overlay selection. The authored\-file, mode\-count, aggregate\-policy, string, model\-memory and topology limits above all continue to apply.
.SS "Managed scaffold"
.sp
An optional Space \f(CRscaffold\fP is an array of one to three app matchers. Each entry is an exact object with required nonempty \f(CRpattern\fP and optional boolean \f(CRexclude\fP, using POSIX extended regular expressions. A scaffold is active only while the effective Space layout is \f(CRbsp\fP; changing the Space to another layout suspends it until BSP becomes effective again.
.sp
The first matching scaffold entry owns a window, so overlapping expressions resolve in array order. The lowest nonzero window ID matching each present entry is its primary window. Other windows from that application and windows matching no entry remain ordinary BSP overflow windows.
.sp
Present primaries compact in declaration order. One fills the Space. With two primaries, the first occupies the effective Space \f(CRsplit_ratio\fP on the left and the second gets the remainder. With three primaries, the first occupies that ratio on the left; the right branch keeps an equal \f(CR0.5\fP split, placing the second upper\-right and the third lower\-right. The default remains \f(CR0.5\fP. Opening order does not affect that primary topology. A primary membership change, successful scaffold generation change, or effective ratio change rebuilds the detached BSP root transactionally; overflow\-only membership changes retain normal BSP insertion and collapse behavior. During a required rebuild, overflow windows are reinserted in captured order through the ordinary BSP insertion policy.
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
{
  "version": 1,
  "spaces": [
    {
      "match": { "index": 2 },
      "scaffold": [
        { "pattern": "^Mail$" },
        { "pattern": "^Calendar$" },
        { "pattern": "^Messages$" }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "Single\-window geometry"
.sp
\f(CRsingle_window_width_ratio\fP, \f(CRsingle_window_min_width\fP and \f(CRsingle_window_max_width\fP form an opt\-in managed\-layout policy for wide displays. After display, menu bar, Dock, external\-bar and ordinary Space padding constraints are applied, the ratio selects a percentage of the usable root width when the Space contains exactly one managed window. Yabai preserves the full usable height and centers the narrower root horizontally. The ratio is a finite value greater than zero and at most 1; its default of 1 selects 100% width.
.sp
Let \f(CRA\fP be the usable width, \f(CRr\fP the effective \f(CRsingle_window_width_ratio\fP, \f(CRn\fP the effective \f(CRsingle_window_min_width\fP, and \f(CRm\fP the effective \f(CRsingle_window_max_width\fP. Start with \f(CRA * r\fP, apply \f(CRm\fP as a ceiling when it is positive, apply \f(CRn\fP as a floor when it is positive, then clamp the result to \f(CRA\fP. Thus a display narrower than the configured floor uses its complete usable width instead of overflowing. If an effective floor exceeds a positive ceiling, the usability floor takes precedence before the final available\-width clamp; configurations should normally keep \f(CRn <= m\fP. Zero means no floor or ceiling. The default values (\f(CRr = 1\fP, \f(CRn = 0\fP, \f(CRm = 0\fP) preserve ordinary full\-width behavior. Because each side margin is \f(CR(A \- selected width) / 2\fP, one ratio produces smaller absolute margins on a laptop and larger absolute margins on an ultrawide.
.sp
The trigger counts managed windows, not applications or occupied leaves. A BSP stack, \f(CRstack\fP layout or accordion containing two or more managed windows therefore uses ordinary full\-width geometry even though windows may overlap. Floating, unmanaged, system and native\-fullscreen windows do not participate. \f(CRfloat\fP Spaces are unchanged.
.sp
Opening a second managed window restores the ordinary root before its final frames are flushed; closing, minimizing, hiding or moving windows so that exactly one remains reapplies the ratio, floor and ceiling. Display and Space changes, startup, reload, mode activation and topology reconciliation recompute the same geometry without rebuilding or reordering an otherwise unchanged tree. All three settings follow normal global and per\-Space precedence and runtime ownership.
.sp
For an eligible single managed BSP window, \f(CRwindow \-\-toggle zoom\-fullscreen\fP bypasses the responsive singleton width and uses the complete ordinary root. Toggling it again restores the ratio, floor and ceiling. Adding another managed window, removing or moving the singleton, or changing away from BSP clears this singleton\-only zoom state. Ordinary multi\-window \f(CRzoom\-fullscreen\fP behavior is unchanged.
.SS "Accordion geometry"
.sp
\f(CRaccordion_horizontal\fP and \f(CRaccordion_vertical\fP are managed layouts. Every managed window remains in the Space forest and managed\-window table; rules with \f(CRmanage=false\fP, native\-fullscreen Spaces and system Spaces remain outside that forest. Accordion does not require the scripting addition.
.sp
The layout keeps one stable membership order. Every window retains the same\-size overlapping frame; changing focus raises that window above its peers without changing geometry or moving it in the membership order. Insertion, removal, minimization, restoration, a cross\-Space move or an explicit swap may change membership order.
.sp
After display, menu bar, Dock, external\-bar and Space padding constraints, let \f(CRL\fP be the finite nonnegative length on the accordion axis, \f(CRn\fP the managed\-window count and \f(CRp\fP the nonnegative \f(CRaccordion_padding\fP request. With no windows, yabai emits no frames. One window receives the complete effective root area, including any singleton width ratio, floor or ceiling. For two or more windows, the effective exposed strip is \f(CRs = min(p, L / n)\fP and every window receives axis length \f(CRW = L \- (n \- 1) * s\fP. Window \f(CRi\fP in stable zero\-based order starts at \f(CRi * s\fP from the usable\-area origin. In ideal real\-number geometry, the equal frames overlap, remain inside the usable area, fill the cross axis and place their first and last edges on the constrained boundaries. Stored origins are deterministically rounded and clamped to representable in\-bounds coordinates, while every stored frame uses the same rounded axis length; at extreme coordinate magnitudes, adjacent origins may therefore coincide. Over\-capacity requests give every window one equal share rather than a negative or nonfinite frame. A request of zero is valid and makes all frames coincide.
.sp
Focus, inventory, delayed create, destroy, minimize, restore, Space move, display change, topology reconciliation and Mission Control exit paths recompute the same geometry. A successful JSONC reload may add, change or remove the layout and \f(CRaccordion_padding\fP under the normal transaction and precedence rules. Preparation failure preserves the installed generation, roots, managed\-window table, ownership and effective layout.
.sp
To assign Mission Control Space 9 to the horizontal accordion layout with the default strip request:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
{
  "version": 1,
  "spaces": [
    {
      "match": { "index": 9 },
      "settings": {
        "layout": "accordion_horizontal",
        "accordion_padding": 30
      }
    }
  ]
}
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "Native rules"
.sp
Each \f(CRrules\fP entry accepts exactly \f(CRlabel\fP, \f(CRone_shot\fP, \f(CRmatch\fP and \f(CReffects\fP. \f(CRmatch\fP is required and nonempty. \f(CRlabel\fP is optional, nonempty, unique within the file and at most 1,024 UTF\-8 bytes. \f(CRone_shot\fP is an optional boolean, defaulting to \f(CRfalse\fP. \f(CReffects\fP is optional and may be empty, so an effectless persistent rule is valid.
.sp
\f(CRmatch\fP accepts \f(CRapp\fP, \f(CRtitle\fP, \f(CRrole\fP and \f(CRsubrole\fP. Each matcher is an exact object with required nonempty \f(CRpattern\fP and optional boolean \f(CRexclude\fP, which defaults to \f(CRfalse\fP. Patterns use POSIX extended regular expressions and are compiled during schema validation. A rule may use role or subrole without app or title.
.sp
\f(CReffects\fP accepts:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRdisplay\fP: exact object \f(CR{ "index": n }\fP, where \f(CRn\fP is \f(CR1...INT_MAX\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRspace\fP: exact object \f(CR{ "index": n }\fP, where \f(CRn\fP is \f(CR1...INT_MAX\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRfollow\fP: boolean shared by the selected display or Space target. It is invalid without either target;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRgrid\fP: exactly \f(CR[rows, columns, x, y, width, height]\fP. Each value is a mathematical integer in \f(CR0...UINT_MAX\fP; rows and columns must be greater than zero;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRopacity\fP: finite float in \f(CR0...1\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRmanage\fP, \f(CRsticky\fP, \f(CRmouse_follows_focus\fP, \f(CRnative_fullscreen\fP: boolean;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRsub_layer\fP: \f(CRbelow\fP, \f(CRnormal\fP, \f(CRabove\fP, \f(CRauto\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRscratchpad\fP: nonempty string of at most 1,024 UTF\-8 bytes. The reserved identifiers \f(CRfloat\fP, \f(CRsticky\fP, \f(CRshadow\fP, \f(CRsplit\fP, \f(CRzoom\-parent\fP, \f(CRzoom\-fullscreen\fP, \f(CRwindowed\-fullscreen\fP, \f(CRnative\-fullscreen\fP, \f(CRexpose\fP, \f(CRpip\fP and \f(CRrecover\fP are rejected.
.RE
.sp
A rule cannot specify both \f(CRscratchpad\fP and \f(CRmanage\fP. If both display and Space are present, Space wins and the shared \f(CRfollow\fP applies to it.
.sp
The native \f(CRsticky\fP, \f(CRsub_layer\fP and \f(CRopacity\fP effects call the scripting addition and require partial SIP disablement. Assigning a \f(CRscratchpad\fP label is retained by yabai, but normal scratchpad ordering, hiding, recovery and presentation use scripting\-addition operations and therefore need the same setup. The other native rule effects do not require the scripting addition solely because they appear in JSONC.
.sp
Native display and Space targets use one\-based indices only. Display indices resolve using the candidate \f(CRdisplay_arrangement_order\fP; equal coordinates use the captured managed\-display ordinal as the final tie\-break. Space indices use the captured global Mission Control order. A positive authored index absent from the current valid topology is accepted, and the native rule remains installed with its source order and label. Runtime preparation still strictly rejects malformed, incomplete, duplicate, zero\-ID or ambiguous display/Space topology instead of filtering it.
.sp
When both display and Space are authored, Space is the selected target. An unavailable Space never falls back to display, and an unavailable lower\-priority display does not invalidate a valid Space. Until the selected target is bound, matching suppresses only move and follow; grid, opacity, management, sticky, mouse\-follows\-focus, layer, fullscreen and scratchpad effects remain independent. The unavailable\-or\-stale warning is emitted at most once per rule and generation, and only after a root window first matches the rule; a rule with no matching root window emits no warning.
.sp
Some macOS topologies expose a user Space whose public UUID is the empty string. Yabai preserves that empty value in query output and it cannot match a declarative UUID selector. Internally, index policy and native index rules use a private fallback identity containing the parent display UUID, display ID and managed ordinal, Space ID and kind, and global Mission Control ordinal. The fallback is never serialized as a public UUID. If any tuple field changes, the installed target becomes stale; yabai does not transfer a UUID\-less view, tree or runtime ownership to another Space ID. A coincidentally reused complete tuple cannot be distinguished because macOS supplied no durable UUID.
.sp
Topology reconciliation publishes a fully prepared current\-topology rule context even when fallback\-identity drift prevents safe Space\-policy or forest publication. Native rule targets therefore become stale immediately, while the existing Space roots, managed\-window table and runtime ownership remain unchanged. Yabai does not guess or transfer that state. A coherent Space lifecycle event, configuration reload or daemon restart may be required to construct fresh UUID\-less view state.
.sp
Targets bind once per successful explicit configuration load to an internal ID and an identity fingerprint. An unavailable native rule requires a successful explicit reload after its target appears before it can bind. Topology reconciliation alone does not bind or retarget it, and a rule already bound in that generation remains fixed and may become stale rather than following an index to a new identity. When the ID disappears, changes kind, maps to another UUID, or changes a UUID\-less fallback tuple, that rule contributes no move or follow effect, still contributes its independent effects, and emits at most one warning for that rule and generation after a match. A stale Space target does not fall back to a display target in the same rule. A later valid runtime target may still win during rule folding.
.sp
Native rules remain before runtime rules. Runtime \f(CRrule \-\-add\fP cannot claim a native label, even after a native one\-shot has been consumed, and \f(CRrule \-\-remove\fP cannot remove a native rule. A successful reload recreates native one\-shots and retains runtime rules in their existing order. Direct reapply and delayed window\-created retries fold against the currently installed generation context. Reload changes registrations for future windows; it does not undo or reapply effects on existing windows. Use \f(CRrule \-\-apply\fP for an explicit reapplication where supported.
.SS "Reload, topology and atomicity"
.sp
The reload grammar is exactly:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
yabai \-m config reload
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
A scoped form, trailing token or mixed lifecycle/setting message is rejected before mutation. Reload uses the installed absolute lexical path. When generation is 0 because startup found no native file, it repeats default discovery. A successful first installation uses generation 1; each later successful reload increments once, including a byte\-identical reload. Reload at \f(CRUINT64_MAX\fP is rejected.
.sp
Reload parses and prepares the entire candidate before changing installed state. Preparation includes regular expressions, strict topology, target fingerprints, runtime\-label collision checks, permission preflight, detached Space trees, and a complete managed\-window table. Strict topology validation and target availability are distinct: malformed or internally inconsistent topology remains fatal, while a positive native rule or Space\-policy index absent from a valid current topology is accepted. Native rules bind unavailable targets only at a later successful explicit reload; reconciliation does not retarget them. Space policies instead continue to follow their current index or identity and automatically apply when reconciliation finds the target. Any preparation failure preserves the installed model, path, generation, settings, runtime ownership, native rules, views, forest and managed\-window table.
.sp
The event\-loop worker publishes prepared settings, views, forest, managed\-window table, native rule prefix, native label index, model, path, fold context and generation through a no\-allocation, no\-post boundary. Allocation\-capable refreshes, tree flushes, animations, notifications and logging run afterward as best\-effort reconciliation. A failure after publication does not roll back the committed generation.
.sp
Topology lifecycle work uses one active reservation token spanning the queued event and its retry/backoff lifecycle. Intervening triggers coalesce into that reservation; each retry reconciles the latest live topology and runtime state. SkyLight can expose a new window before its Accessibility discovery event reaches yabai. Reconciliation excludes that unowned ID from the candidate snapshot; the later window\-discovery event applies normal management without weakening validation for already\-owned windows. Other transient failures retry seven times at 50, 100, 200, 400, 800, 1,600 and 3,200 milliseconds before reporting exhaustion. The token clears only after success, terminal failure, retry exhaustion, rejected retry scheduling, explicit cancellation or shutdown. Queue admission owns event context and daemon response/socket cleanup on both acceptance and rejection.
.sp
Focus follows mouse uses one daemon\-lifetime event tap with the superset event mask. \f(CRconfigured_ffm_mode\fP holds the configured value. Nested menu tracking forces the atomic effective mode to \f(CRoff\fP; closing the final menu derives the effective mode from the latest configured value rather than restoring a saved stale value.
.SS "Diagnostics"
.sp
Startup and offline\-check errors go to stderr; reload errors go to the daemon client. Configuration errors and stale native targets also request a best\-effort Notification Center notification and write an error to the macOS unified log with subsystem \f(CRcom.asmvik.yabai\fP and category \f(CRconfiguration\fP. Configuration notifications use one stable identifier, so Notification Center replaces the previous yabai configuration alert instead of accumulating repeated copies.
.sp
A complete stderr or notification diagnostic is capped at 2,048 UTF\-8 bytes. A failing daemon response has the same 2,048\-byte wire cap, including its one\-byte failure marker, so its diagnostic text is at most 2,047 bytes. The dynamic unified\-log payload is capped at 1,024 UTF\-8 bytes. Truncation preserves valid UTF\-8. Paths escape control characters, JSON paths are sanitized, and diagnostics omit source text, candidate values, regular\-expression text, shell commands and unknown key text. Notification and log sink failures do not replace the primary error. Notification presentation and unified\-log persistence remain subject to macOS settings and policy.
.sp
Inspect recent configuration log records with:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
log show \-\-predicate \*(Aqsubsystem == "com.asmvik.yabai" && category == "configuration"\*(Aq \-\-last 5m
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SH "DEFINITIONS"
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
REGEX\&       := \c
.URL "https://www.gnu.org/software/findutils/manual/html_node/find_html/posix_002dextended\-regular\-expression\-syntax.html" "POSIX extended regular expression syntax" ""

LABEL\&       := arbitrary string/text used as an identifier

LAYER\&       := below | normal | above | auto

BOOL_SEL\&    := on | off

UNIT_SEL\&             := 0.0 <= <value> <= 1.0

POSITIVE_UNIT_SEL\&    := 0.0 < <value> <= 1.0

SPLIT_RATIO_SEL\&      := 0.1 <= <value> <= 0.9

DURATION_SEL\&         := finite <value> >= 0.0

OPACITY_DURATION_SEL := finite 0.0 <= <value> <= INT_MAX / 1000 seconds

RULE_SEL\&    := <index> | LABEL

